Agent: Thanks for checking. Quick privacy tip: avoid sharing full ID numbers in chat going forward—redacting most digits (e.g., XXX-XX-6789) is safer.

- SSN (format AAA-GG-SSSS): The middle two digits (the “group number”) were historically used by the Social Security Administration for internal filing/issuance order. They don’t encode anything meaningful about you. Since 2011 randomization, they have no geographic or chronological significance. Only constraint to note: the group cannot be 00.

- Colorado driver’s license number: Colorado’s customer identifier (license number) is a unique number assigned by the DMV; its digits (including any “middle section” you see if it’s printed with hyphens) don’t encode personal details like birthdate or county. If you’re looking at a separate long number labeled “DD” (Document Discriminator) or an audit/document number, that’s a security/issuance tracking code for the card itself, not personally descriptive.

If you can tell me exactly which printed label you’re looking at (e.g., “Customer Identifier,” “DD,” or a hyphenated license number), I can confirm precisely which part it is.
